    related: [
      { collection: 'reviews', slug: 'wolverine-marvel' },
      { collection: 'news', slug: 'interview-insomniac-games-over-wolverine-reviews-openwereldgames-en-de-grote-lek' },
    ],
    seo: {
      title: 'Review: Wolverine is a fantastically made game, but not a fantastic game',
      description:
        'Marvel’s Wolverine on PS5 scores 6.8: near perfect tech and acting, thin combat, little enemy variety, awful pacing.',
    },
    body: [
      'Score: 6.8 out of 10. Plus: technically almost perfect; a solid story with excellent acting. Minus: middling gameplay; barely any enemy variety; terrible pacing.',
      'Wolverine is a decent game, but never more than that. Top notch graphics and acting lift it a little above average, but we are talking about a game, and in this medium those things are secondary. What it does turn on, the gameplay, lacks the sauce and the salt to get near what we are used to from Insomniac.',
      'It is easy to imagine a less hardcore audience will still have a fine time with the icon hairy down to his lower back. Wolverine is one of the most popular superheroes, and the genuinely solid story you walk through is packed with insanely tight performances from every actor. Liam McIntyre deserves the biggest applause here, not because the Wolverine actor stands head and shoulders above the rest, but because he manages to step out of the shadow of Hugh Jackman (forever the only real Wolverine). Troy Baker lays down a fine Essex, and Krizia Bajos is probably my favourite version of Jean Grey ever.',
      'Technical perfection. Technically it is enough to make you drool until you drown. You do not make a game this pretty unless you put one of the best studios in the world to work for years with an almost endless budget. And so it happened. No ugly unfinished corner, no framedrop in sight, and the way Wolverine comes out of a fight with half torn strips of flesh, adamantium ribs sometimes fully visible, is a feat.',
      'And all that splendour is not limited to the linear levels. In larger, detailed environments such as Japan or the fictional Madripoor, no stone or leaf is left untouched. The coat this game wears is as clean as it gets, even when it is soaked from head to toe in flying blood. With a critical eye you could still say it is too clean, because technically impressive or not, a more pronounced, stylistic face or art direction would not have been out of place. Over time that handsome face even becomes a bit… boring. At least it is consistent, because that stamp also sits on the gameplay.',
      'Leave the wheel alone. For all the positive presentation, in the end it is little more than dressing to disguise a simple button masher action game from 2004 as something of this moment. By the time the credits hit, in about ten hours you have mashed square about 10,000 times, and triangle 5,000 times. The first for normal attacks, the second to leap at one of the many enemies. Those enemies, 90 percent of the time, are either soldiers or soldiers in a ninja costume. That dumb mashing is dutifully mixed with blocking or dodging attacks, but those kinds of systems live or die by the feel those actions give you.',
      'The odd thing is Insomniac never needed to reinvent this wheel. Pretty much every action game, from Bayonetta to Metal Gear Rising and Sekiro, or modern peers such as the just released Onimusha, has this trick worked out to perfection. Wolverine learns nothing from them. Dodging an attack never feels like more than a slightly dumb roll away, while the weird timing of the parry only rarely delivers anything satisfying.',
      'Fine. There are large stretches in Wolverine where the game gets away with mindless mashing. Do not get me wrong, the game is not bad. And the more casual player probably has less experience with similar games that execute almost everything here far better. But anyone who knows the genre regularly sees “mindless fun” turn into simply “mindless”. It does not help that the game constantly hits the brakes. Because it wants you to walk slowly, so there is breathing room for the story. Because it wants you to hunt drawings for a young mutant for, uh, reasons that are undoubtedly very good. Because it worries for the umpteenth time about the character growth of the leads, instead of giving priority to just being a fun game. We are used to Insomniac mastering both.',
      'Normally it is easy to judge (condemn?) a game like this much more harshly, and anyone who believes in the adage “gameplay is king” should stay well away. But Wolverine saves a lot with its journey and attention to detail. The storyline that combines Indiana Jones with the X-Men universe stays tight, a handful of more unique scenes steal the show, and despite the simple nature of the gameplay, I always wanted to continue to see what the next chapter had. Added up that makes a decent ride. But over the top of it sits a thought that will not be shaken from start to finish: this would have been a better comic, film or series than a game.',
      'Marvel’s Wolverine comes out on 15 September for PlayStation 5.',
      'What kind of game is Marvel’s Wolverine? Marvel’s Wolverine is a single player action adventure for PlayStation 5. The game turns on fast close combat, story driven missions and Logan’s violent fighting style. So do not expect an open world game in the style of a big sandbox, but a focused superhero action game with cinematic scenes, familiar Marvel characters and a lot of emphasis on melee fights.',
      'How much does Marvel’s Wolverine cost on PS5? Marvel’s Wolverine has a European recommended price of 79.99 euro for the standard edition. Digital extras or fancier editions can cost more, depending on the version and shop. For most players the standard edition is enough, because it contains the full game. Extra editions are mainly interesting if you care about cosmetic bonuses or digital collectible content.',
      'How does Marvel’s Wolverine sit next to Marvel’s Spider-Man? Marvel’s Wolverine and Marvel’s Spider-Man both come from Insomniac Games, but they aim at a different feel. Spider-Man turns strongly on acrobatics, web swinging and open city exploration, while Wolverine leans heavier on brute close combat and a darker tone. Anyone mainly looking for the smooth freedom of Spider-Man should therefore count on a more compact and harder action adventure.',
      'Why is Marvel’s Wolverine not meant for young children? Marvel’s Wolverine clearly aims at an older audience through the violence, blood and hard tone. The European PEGI rating is 18, which means the game is not meant for minors. That fits the character, but makes the game less suitable as a general family superhero game. Parents are better off checking gameplay footage and the age rating first.',
    ],
